<title>UK: BA March traffic up, load factor down.</title>
<headline>BA March traffic up, load factor down.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-04-03</dateline>
<text>
<p>British Airways Plc said on Thursday its paying passenger traffic in March rose 7.3 percent, while passenger load factor fell 0.9 points to 76.2 percent versus the year-ago month.</p>
<p>Britain's national flag carrier said mainline scheduled available seat kilometres rose by 8.7 percent.</p>
<p>For the three months to March 31, mainline scheduled traffic grew by 7.7 percent with premium growth of 3.3 percent compared against non-premium growth of 8.6 percent over last year.  </p>
<p>British Airways operating statistics</p>
<p>*March 1997 vs 1996</p>
<p>Passengers carried (millions)</p>
<p>UK/Europe - 1.875 vs 1.857 (up 1.0 pct)</p>
<p>Intercontinental - 1.010 vs 927 (up 8.9 pct)</p>
<p>Total - 2.885 vs 2.784 (up 3.6 pct) Traffic (millions Revenue</p>
<p>Passenger Kilometres)</p>
<p>UK/Europe - 1,649 vs 1,573 (up 4.8 pct)</p>
<p>Intercontinental - 7,325 vs 6,787 (up 7.9 pct)</p>
<p>Total - 8,974 vs 8,360 (up 7.3 pct)</p>
<p>Capacity (millions Available Seat Kilometres)</p>
<p>UK/Europe - 2,379 vs 2,253 (up 5.6 pct)</p>
<p>Intercontinental - 9,405 vs 8,589 (up 9.5 pct)</p>
<p>Total - 11,785 vs 10,843 (up 8.7 pct)</p>
<p>Passenger load factor</p>
<p>UK/Europe - 69.3 percent vs 69.8 percent</p>
<p>Intercontinental - 77.9 percent vs 79.0 percent</p>
<p>Total - 76.2 percent vs 77.1 percent</p>
